# Claudia demo — the scenario, single source of truth (~90 s at 2×, one act).
#
# This file IS the take: demo/drive-take.sh reads it line by line and performs it
# against the real claude TUI (and `npm run demo:record` is you performing it by
# hand — same script, human tempo). One continuing session with Agnès, the
# fictional persona in vault/ — the model's replies vary between takes; the
# fixture guarantees the material they draw on is identical every time.
#
# The take types no slash command. She launches claude, says the name, and talks.
#
# Verbs (one per line; full-line # comments and blank lines are ignored):
#   type <text>            type it like a person (char by char), then Enter
#   draft <text>           same, but WITHOUT Enter — a multi-line prompt in progress
#   newline                soft newline inside the prompt (backslash + Enter)
#   send                   validate whatever the drafts have built
#   settle                 wait until claude finishes the turn (pane stops repainting;
#                          FAILS the take on a permission prompt — rig must be pre-allowed)
#   expect <regex> [secs]  wait until the pane matches (space-free regex; default 90s)
#   sleep <secs>           fixed pause — a beat of stillness reads well on video
#   picker-enter           if a skill picker is open, take the highlighted option
#                          (fails the take on a permission prompt instead)
#   key <tmux-key>         send a raw key (e.g. C-d)
#
# Validate edits without recording: DRY_RUN=1 ./demo/drive-take.sh

# — Shot 1 · cold open: an empty prompt breathing one beat, then claude
sleep 2
type claude
expect Claude.Code 60
sleep 3

# — Shot 2 · naming her is the entire trigger — no command, no menu.
# The greeting comes back with her name, her husband's name and the follow-up
# left open three days ago. None of it was typed here; it came from the vault.
type Claudia, I need to talk
settle
sleep 2

# — Shot 3 · the grievance, in three breaths. One prompt, three lines: the
# absurd duration first, the derisory object last. Bacri-Jaoui's whole engine.
draft Forty minutes!
newline
sleep 1
draft We argued for forty minutes!!!
newline
sleep 1
draft about a window.
send
settle
sleep 2

# — Shot 4 · the punchline. The reasonable half, a beat of silence, then the
# real reason escaping in capitals. The SILENCE is the joke — which is why this
# is drafted line by line and not typed in one go.
draft I wanted him to notice I was cold
newline
sleep 2
draft BEFORE I HAD TO ASK!!!
send
settle
sleep 3

# — Shot 5 · she concedes to being named, and pulls the keep herself.
# No guard here on purpose: "you got me" answers any reflective reply, and
# reflecting the pattern is Claudia's baseline — an earlier take failed on an
# `expect (telepathy|…)` while she had in fact named it perfectly, in her own
# words ("there it is again — you wanted to be noticed before you had to ask").
# A regex cannot tell a reflection from a non-sequitur; the guard that matters
# is the one below, on which sentence gets kept.
draft ok, ok. you got me.
newline
sleep 1
# Two failure modes seen across takes, both closed by this one line: a bare "keep
# that one" once kept CLAUDIA's preceding sentence ("mine, not yours"), and once
# the model tidied the caps and the "!!!" away ("word for word" forbids that).
draft keep that — mine, word for word, caps and all 📌
send

# — Shot 6 · the keep. Whether the choice UI (ADR-0024) opens is the model's call;
# `picker-enter` is a no-op when no picker is up, so the take survives both paths.
settle
sleep 2
picker-enter
settle
# The one assertion that matters: HER sentence, verbatim, is now in the file. The
# pane can't witness it (her own message shows the same words higher up).
expect-file .claudia/keepsakes.md BEFORE.I.HAD.TO.ASK

# — Shot 7 · the last frame: her sentence written into keepsakes.md, in plain
# markdown, attributed to her. Held long — the site plays this cast at 2×.
sleep 12
